---
import Page from "../components/Page.astro";
---

<Page title="Method" description="Where Options Lab's figures come from: the prices it reads, the formula it uses and how each figure is computed.">
	<div class="max-w-3xl">
		<h1 class="text-[2rem] font-medium leading-tight tracking-tight text-ink-strong">Sources and method</h1>
		<p class="mt-4 text-ink">
			Every figure is computed by code from the prices of the options, the same way for every ticker. No one picks a
			contract or a strategy, and no AI writes anything on these pages.
		</p>

		<h2 class="sec sec-title mt-12">Sources</h2>
		<ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-ink">
			<li><strong class="font-medium text-ink-strong">Yahoo Finance</strong>: each option's bid, ask, last trade, volume and open interest; the underlying's price, dividend and next results date; a year of daily closes; and the yield of the 13-week Treasury bill. Option prices are delayed about 15 minutes, and outside market hours some quotes read zero.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 class="sec sec-title mt-12">The price of a contract</h2>
		<p class="mt-3 text-ink">
			A contract is marked at the middle of its bid and ask. When it has no quote (outside market hours, often) it is marked
			at its last trade, which may be old. Everything else about the contract is read from that mark.
		</p>

		<h2 class="sec sec-title mt-12">Implied volatility and greeks</h2>
		<p class="mt-3 text-ink">
			The formula is Black-Scholes-Merton: the underlying's price, the strike, the time to expiration (to 4 p.m. New York
			time on the day), the Treasury bill's yield as the risk-free rate and the trailing dividend as a continuous yield.
			The implied volatility is the one at which the formula gives the mark. US equity options are American, and can be
			exercised before expiration; the formula is the European one, the usual way a chain is read. The greeks come from
			the same formula at that volatility: delta and gamma per $1 of the underlying, theta per calendar day, vega per
			volatility point and rho per percentage point of the rate. A contract whose mark is under what the formula gives at
			no volatility has no implied volatility.
		</p>

		<h2 class="sec sec-title mt-12">Odds</h2>
		<p class="mt-3 text-ink">
			The odds are the ones the option prices imply, in the formula's risk-neutral world: the underlying grows at the
			rate less the dividend and ends spread as a lognormal at the implied volatility. They are not a forecast of what
			will happen. "Ends in the money" is the chance the underlying ends past the strike at expiration. "Touches the
			strike" is twice that, capped at 100%: the usual approximation of the chance it gets there at any point before.
		</p>

		<h2 class="sec sec-title mt-12">An expiration as a whole</h2>
		<ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-ink">
			<li><strong class="font-medium text-ink-strong">At-the-money IV</strong>: the smile read from the out-of-the-money side (puts under the price, calls over it), interpolated at the price.</li>
			<li><strong class="font-medium text-ink-strong">Expected move</strong>: the price of the straddle at the strike nearest the price, a call and a put together. It is what the market charges for the move, not a prediction of it.</li>
			<li><strong class="font-medium text-ink-strong">One-sigma range</strong>: the prices between which the underlying ends with a 68% chance, by the at-the-money volatility.</li>
			<li><strong class="font-medium text-ink-strong">25-delta skew</strong>: the implied volatility of the put whose delta is −0.25 less that of the call whose delta is 0.25, in volatility points, each interpolated by delta.</li>
			<li><strong class="font-medium text-ink-strong">Max pain</strong>: the strike at which the options open today would pay their holders least if the underlying ended there.</li>
			<li><strong class="font-medium text-ink-strong">IV 30 days</strong>: at-the-money volatility interpolated at 30 days, in total variance, between the expirations around it.</li>
			<li><strong class="font-medium text-ink-strong">Realised volatility</strong>: the annualised standard deviation of the last 20, 60 and 250 daily log returns of the underlying.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 class="sec sec-title mt-12">Strategies</h2>
		<p class="mt-3 text-ink">
			A strategy is up to eight legs: calls, puts and shares, long or short. A contract is 100 shares. A leg without a
			price opens at today's mark; one with a price opens there. The profit and loss is drawn at the first expiration of
			the strategy, today, and on a date between the two. Before an expiration, each option is valued with the formula at
			its own implied volatility (or the at-the-money one, when it has none), moved by the volatility shift chosen.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-3 text-ink">
			Breakevens, the most and the least are read at the first expiration over prices from nearly zero to three times the
			highest strike. When the position is long calls or shares that nothing caps, its profit has no limit; when it is
			short them uncovered, its loss has none. With legs that expire later, the most and the least are read from those
			prices and are an approximation.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-3 text-ink">
			The chance of profit is the chance, by the at-the-money volatility of the first expiration, that the underlying
			ends where the strategy shows a profit at that expiration: the odds as described above, summed between breakevens.
			Margin, commissions, early assignment and taxes are not counted.
		</p>

		<h2 class="sec sec-title mt-12">What is kept</h2>
		<p class="mt-3 text-ink">
			Only the strategies a user saves: their name, ticker, legs and dates, in Market Hub's database under that user's
			account. They are deleted with the account.
		</p>
	</div>
</Page>
